  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Repeating to soften: reduplication",
     "texto": [
      "Vietnamese has a resource that Spanish doesn't have: **repeating** a word (sometimes changing its tone or ending a little) to give it a nuance. It's called **từ láy**.",
      "**With adjectives, it softens**: «a bit…, somewhat…, -ish». **nhỏ → nho nhỏ** (tiny), **đẹp → đèm đẹp** (pretty, nice), **xanh → xanh xanh** (greenish, bluish), **nhẹ → nhè nhẹ** (gentle, soft), **chậm → chầm chậm** (slowly), **vàng → vàng vàng** (yellowish), **buồn → buồn buồn** (a bit sad).",
      "Watch the tone: often the **first** syllable changes to a level or falling tone («đèm», «nhè», «chầm») and the **second** keeps the original.",
      "**With time words, it generalises**: **ngày ngày** (day after day), **người người** (everyone), **đêm đêm** (night after night).",
      "**With verbs, it repeats the action gently**: **gật gật** (nodding several times), **cười cười** (smiling a little).",
      "There are many words like this that come ready-made and cannot be taken apart: **lung linh** (sparkling), **nhộn nhịp** (bustling), **thong thả** (unhurried), **lặng lẽ** (silently). They add colour to descriptions: they are the mark of someone who writes well."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
